  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does the coil in a circuit breaker work?",
      "a": "All the current passes through a small coil, making it an electromagnet. Normal current gives a weak pull, but a sudden big current gives a pull strong enough to yank the latch almost instantly.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the magnet in a breaker do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the latch in a circuit breaker do?",
      "a": "The latch holds a loaded spring back while all is normal. When the coil, or a bending bimetal strip, knocks it, it lets go and the spring takes over.",
      "asks": [
        "What holds a breaker on?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is there a spring in a circuit breaker?",
      "a": "Switching the breaker on loads a spring. When the latch releases, the stored energy flings the contacts apart fast, which keeps sparking to a minimum.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes a breaker snap off?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I reset a tripped breaker?",
      "a": "Push the switch fully back to on, which closes the contacts and reloads the spring. If it trips again at once, there is still a fault, so unplug things and call an electrician if it keeps happening.",
      "asks": [
        "What does it mean when the breaker trips?",
        "Why does my breaker keep tripping?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between a fuse and a circuit breaker?",
      "a": "Both cut the current when it gets too big. A fuse melts and must be replaced, while a breaker is a switch you can reset. Many breakers also have a bimetal strip that bends to catch slower overloads.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a breaker better than a fuse?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Many circuit breakers combine a magnetic coil for sudden faults with a bimetal strip for slower overloads.",
    "Breakers are rated in amps, for example 6 amps for lighting circuits and 32 amps for socket circuits in many UK homes."
  ],
